      "text": "In order to solve this, we will first use the Law of Cosines to obtain the length of side $BC$ and then use the Law of Cosines to obtain the length of sides $AD$ and $AE$. Then, since triangle $D$ and $E$ are the trisectors, we will use the Law of Cosines again to get the following equations:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos \\angle DEA &= \\frac{AD^2 + DE^2 - AE^2}{2AD \\cdot DE}, \\\\\n\\cos \\angle DAE &= \\cos \\angle DEA(1)\\cos \\angle ADE(2).\n\\end{align*}\nHowever, in order to solve for $\\cos \\angle ADE$, we note that triangle $A$ doesn't have any right triangles, so $\\sin ^2 \\angle ADE + \\cos ^2 \\angle ADE = 1$. This gives us that\n\\[\\cos \\angle ADE = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin ^ 2 \\angle ADE}.\\]\nSubstituting this into equation $(2)$, we get that $\\cos \\angle DAE = \\frac{AD - AE}{\\sqrt{3}AE}$. Now, knowing the length of $BC$, $AD$, and $AE$, we can easily solve for $\\cos \\angle DAE$: $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}.$",
